<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Yaroslavl</h1><p class="st-meta">1218 CE – 1463 CE · capital Yaroslavl</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Volga principality around the city founded by Yaroslav the Wise, ruled by its own princes until it passed to Moscow in 1463.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a> was founded in the early 11th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> at the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kotorosl">Kotorosl</a>; a legend has him killing a bear there. In 1218 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-of-rostov">Konstantin Vsevolodovich of Rostov</a> gave it to his son Vsevolod, who founded a separate princely line.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its position on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a> a trading town. Vsevolod was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> on the Sit in 1238. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-the-black">Fyodor the Black</a>, who ruled from the 1260s, spent years at the court of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, married a daughter of the khan and was later venerated as a saint. The principality later split into many small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Its princes increasingly served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>, and in 1463 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> annexed the principality. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a> princes survived as noble families such as the Shakhovskoy and Kurbsky. The city became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia's</a> richest commercial centres in the 17th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-yaroslavl.jpg" alt="Principality of Yaroslavl" width="320" height="289"><figcaption>Image: Hellerick · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rostov_Yaroslavl_Beloozero_Galich_northern_Vladimir_14th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1218 CE – 1463 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Yaroslavl</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vsevolod Konstantinovich, Fyodor the Black</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Iaroslavl Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1340&amp;focus=principality-of-yaroslavl" aria-label="Principality of Yaroslavl on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5534 227 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Yaroslavl lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5854 483L5878 523M5854 483L6352 637M5854 483L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5878 523h0M6352 637h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5854 483h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5854 483h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Yaroslavl. <a href="/atlas?year=1340&amp;focus=principality-of-yaroslavl">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
